  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x^2 + 0.5 z^2) / 2, drawn at half its value in height. It is steeper along x (left-right), so plain steps overshoot there once the learning rate passes 2/2.4 ≈ 0.83, and swings grow until the run diverges.",
    "Each step moves the ball by -learning rate × (slope + noise). The noise is random, centred on zero, with size 1.5 × sqrt((64 − b)/(63 b)) for batch b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64. It comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same noise.",
    "The purple arrow is the mini-batch step the ball will take next; the dark arrow is the step the full data would give. Both are drawn at 5 times their real length (one scale for both); a zero step is hidden.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or is marked diverged when the loss passes 5 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl; the ball and path are then drawn at the rim, while the loss readout keeps its true value.",
    "The trail shows the start point plus one point per step (up to 41 points); the ghost keeps the last run's path the same way. The ball takes 5 steps per second at 1× speed.",
    "With noise, a single step lowers the loss only on average; small batches can raise it on some steps and keep wandering near the lowest point."
  ]
}